#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e79a91b-c479-53da-9f06-59c082fa4187" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4352698-7af7-5d74-9c3a-0f951c3e6078" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075033 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve, *without* using graphs, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=4 \\\\ x-2 &=y\\,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order to solve the system without a graph, we will need to combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation.\n\nOne way to combine the equations is called the **substitution method.** To use the substitution method, we substitute for one variable in terms of the other.\n\nFor instance, notice that we can make $y$ the subject of the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\color{red}y=x - 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $y$ into the first equation, we get an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+(x-2) & =4 \\\\ 2x-2 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3.$ But we also need to figure out what $y$ is. We can do this by substituting $x=3$ back into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-2 \\\\ y & =3-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 3$ and $y = 1$, which can be written as $\\left(3, 1\\right).$\n\n**Note:** Whenever we isolate the variable in one equation, we have to substitute it into the *other* equation. We cannot substitute a variable into the same equation it came from, because this will not give us any more information about the variable." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1e8cb2d-3d4e-5cb9-8947-2bd9deb11b89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8131 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
